1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,776
At the Corner Cafe, Asha's module from
Chapter 18 checks itself.

2
00:00:04,796 --> 00:00:06,410
Each check prints True.

3
00:00:06,433 --> 00:00:11,068
But one day, a change breaks a tool, and a
check prints False.

4
00:00:11,088 --> 00:00:12,795
The program carries on.

5
00:00:12,815 --> 00:00:14,522
Nobody reads that line.

6
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:20,440
This is Code Quest, Chapter 19: tests that
stop loudly when something breaks.

7
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:25,923
And an AI assistant will write tests for
Asha, and promise they catch any mistake.

8
00:00:25,943 --> 00:00:26,591
Will they?

9
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:29,930
You will find out by breaking the code on
purpose.

10
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:33,687
Here is today's mission: Asha's test file.

11
00:00:33,707 --> 00:00:36,040
Line 2 imports her three tools.

12
00:00:36,067 --> 00:00:38,097
Each block below is one test.

13
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:42,197
One command runs them all, and names any
tool that breaks.

14
00:00:42,217 --> 00:00:43,258
Let's build it.

15
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:49,947
Your goals: write an assert, write and run
a pytest file, read its report, test edge

16
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:53,151
cases and decimals, and check an AI's
tests.

17
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:59,264
A test runs your code with a known input,
and checks the result against the answer

18
00:00:59,284 --> 00:01:00,006
you expect.

19
00:01:00,026 --> 00:01:03,110
If the result is wrong, the test fails
loudly.

20
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:04,930
Tests keep code working.

21
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:10,454
Every time you, a colleague or an AI
changes it, you run the tests again.

22
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,129
Every test has three parts.

23
00:01:13,149 --> 00:01:15,228
A known input: 40 biscuits.

24
00:01:15,248 --> 00:01:17,715
A run of the code: boxes needed.

25
00:01:17,735 --> 00:01:19,737
And a comparison: is it 4?

26
00:01:19,757 --> 00:01:20,903
Yes means pass.

27
00:01:20,923 --> 00:01:21,991
No means fail.

28
00:01:22,587 --> 00:01:25,032
There are three ways to check a result.

29
00:01:25,052 --> 00:01:27,877
A printed check prints False, and carries
on.

30
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,760
An assert stops the program with an error.

31
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,646
And a pytest test runs every test, then
names each one that failed.

32
00:01:36,253 --> 00:01:39,812
Think of a test as a smoke alarm in the
cafe kitchen.

33
00:01:39,832 --> 00:01:42,513
It stays quiet while everything is fine.

34
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:46,083
And it makes a loud noise the moment
something goes wrong.

35
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:49,140
The comparison stops working at one point.

36
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,447
An alarm watches the whole room.

37
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:54,329
A test checks only the inputs you wrote.

38
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:55,610
Quick check.

39
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:59,039
Which of these stops the program when the
result is wrong?

40
00:02:10,413 --> 00:02:10,532
C.

41
00:02:10,552 --> 00:02:11,019
Assert.

42
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:15,812
A printed check only prints False, and a
comment does nothing at all.

43
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:17,476
Take a breather.

44
00:02:17,496 --> 00:02:20,837
Next, Asha sees why a printed check is not
enough.

45
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:26,155
Step 1.

46
00:02:26,175 --> 00:02:30,466
Someone typed 1.01 as the tax rate,
instead of 1.1.

47
00:02:30,486 --> 00:02:31,733
Look at line 2.

48
00:02:31,767 --> 00:02:35,819
Line 5 checks the tea price with print.

49
00:02:35,839 --> 00:02:38,326
Line 6 saves the report.

50
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:51,357
False, then Report saved.

51
00:02:51,377 --> 00:02:56,039
The check found the problem, but the
program saved the report anyway.

52
00:02:56,653 --> 00:02:57,060
Step 2.

53
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:00,597
An assertion is a statement that a
condition must be true.

54
00:03:00,617 --> 00:03:02,853
You write assert, then the condition.

55
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:04,870
Here the tax rate is right.

56
00:03:04,890 --> 00:03:10,620
Both conditions are true, so assert does
nothing, and line 7 saves the report.

57
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:15,698
But when a condition is false, assert
stops the program with an Assertion Error.

58
00:03:15,718 --> 00:03:18,034
After a comma, you can add a message.

59
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:21,293
Now the wrong rate again, on line 2.

60
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:25,905
Line 5 checks the tea price with assert,
and adds a message.

61
00:03:25,925 --> 00:03:27,741
Line 6 saves the report.

62
00:03:39,113 --> 00:03:39,327
No.

63
00:03:39,347 --> 00:03:43,603
Line 5 stops the program, so the report is
never saved.

64
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:49,215
And the message says exactly what was
wrong: tea with tax should be 3.85.

65
00:03:49,817 --> 00:03:50,293
Step 3.

66
00:03:50,313 --> 00:03:52,634
pytest is a popular testing tool.

67
00:03:52,654 --> 00:03:58,237
It does not come with Python, so you
install it once with pip, Python's package

68
00:03:58,257 --> 00:03:58,947
installer.

69
00:03:58,967 --> 00:04:03,052
On macOS and Linux, type python3 instead
of python.

70
00:04:03,072 --> 00:04:06,997
You will learn more about packages in
Chapter 24.

71
00:04:07,033 --> 00:04:09,447
Then you run a test file like this.

72
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:12,853
The dash q option asks for a short, quiet
report.

73
00:04:13,453 --> 00:04:17,693
Here is Asha's module from Chapter 18:
with tax, and money.

74
00:04:17,733 --> 00:04:21,794
She adds boxes needed, from the AI section
of Chapter 18.

75
00:04:21,814 --> 00:04:24,443
Line 18 rounds up with math dot ceil.

76
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:29,013
And she removes the old printed checks
from the bottom of the file.

77
00:04:29,033 --> 00:04:32,966
Tests will replace them, so the module now
prints nothing.

78
00:04:33,587 --> 00:04:35,718
Here is the safe rule for names.

79
00:04:35,738 --> 00:04:40,490
Every test is a function whose name starts
with test and an underscore.

80
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:46,137
And the file name starts the same way, so
pytest finds it when it searches a folder.

81
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:48,548
Line 2 imports the three tools.

82
00:04:48,568 --> 00:04:52,809
Each test has one assert, with an exact
expected value.

83
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,584
Three tests, and all three pass.

84
00:05:06,604 --> 00:05:08,780
Each dot is one passing test.

85
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:13,006
The last line counts them, and shows how
long the run took.

86
00:05:13,026 --> 00:05:15,226
That time changes on every run.

87
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:16,616
Quick check.

88
00:05:16,636 --> 00:05:19,405
Which of these functions will pytest run?

89
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:30,917
D.

90
00:05:30,937 --> 00:05:31,626
Test tea.

91
00:05:31,646 --> 00:05:35,090
Its name starts with test and an
underscore.

92
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:38,396
Tea test ends with it, so pytest skips it.

93
00:05:38,987 --> 00:05:39,552
Breather.

94
00:05:39,572 --> 00:05:44,364
Next, Asha breaks her module on purpose,
to see what a failure looks like.

95
00:05:49,117 --> 00:05:49,677
Step 4.

96
00:05:49,697 --> 00:05:53,826
Asha copies her files into a folder called
broken.

97
00:05:53,846 --> 00:05:58,555
In the copy, line 18 uses round instead of
math dot ceil.

98
00:05:58,575 --> 00:06:01,957
That is the AI's mistake from Chapter 18.

99
00:06:02,553 --> 00:06:07,989
She runs the same three tests on the
broken copy, and adds dash dash t b equals

100
00:06:08,009 --> 00:06:09,923
short, for a shorter report.

101
00:06:09,943 --> 00:06:13,306
Remember: round goes to the nearest whole
number.

102
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:25,669
The third one.

103
00:06:25,689 --> 00:06:28,480
The first line shows two dots and an F.

104
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,210
F means one test failed.

105
00:06:30,233 --> 00:06:33,993
The report names the test, and its line:
14.

106
00:06:34,033 --> 00:06:37,360
The line that starts with E shows the
values.

107
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:41,004
Boxes needed returned 3, but the test
expected 4.

108
00:06:41,024 --> 00:06:44,499
The last line counts one failed and two
passed.

109
00:06:45,083 --> 00:06:45,562
Step 5.

110
00:06:45,582 --> 00:06:51,043
An edge case is an unusual input at the
limit of what a function must handle.

111
00:06:51,067 --> 00:06:57,022
For boxes, the edges are no biscuits,
exactly one full box, and one biscuit more

112
00:06:57,042 --> 00:06:57,844
than a box.

113
00:06:57,864 --> 00:07:00,683
On the working module, all three pass.

114
00:07:01,287 --> 00:07:04,686
Now Asha runs the edge cases on the broken
copy.

115
00:07:04,706 --> 00:07:08,176
Remember: round goes to the nearest whole
number.

116
00:07:19,547 --> 00:07:19,775
13.

117
00:07:19,795 --> 00:07:25,066
13 divided by 12 is about 1.08, and round
turns that into 1 box.

118
00:07:25,086 --> 00:07:26,720
The test expected 2.

119
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:28,787
The other two still pass.

120
00:07:28,833 --> 00:07:33,140
So good tests check normal values and edge
values.

121
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:36,948
Each catches mistakes from a different
side.

122
00:07:37,553 --> 00:07:38,088
Step 6.

123
00:07:38,108 --> 00:07:43,711
In Chapter 2 you saw that point 1, three
times, is not exactly point 3.

124
00:07:43,731 --> 00:07:46,483
Here are three tests of that total.

125
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:50,478
Line 12 rounds the total to two decimal
places.

126
00:07:50,498 --> 00:07:56,635
Line 17 uses pytest dot approx, which
accepts a value that is very close.

127
00:08:08,013 --> 00:08:10,321
The first one, test exact.

128
00:08:10,341 --> 00:08:13,723
The total is a tiny bit more than 0.3.

129
00:08:13,767 --> 00:08:15,113
The other two pass.

130
00:08:15,133 --> 00:08:19,430
For decimals, round the result to two
places, or use approx.

131
00:08:20,017 --> 00:08:20,516
Step 7.

132
00:08:20,536 --> 00:08:24,892
The cafe owner wants every price label to
start with U S D.

133
00:08:24,912 --> 00:08:29,640
Before Asha changes money, she writes the
test for the new rule.

134
00:08:41,013 --> 00:08:43,880
No, and that is what she expects.

135
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:47,203
The money function still returns 4.00.

136
00:08:47,233 --> 00:08:50,307
The minus line shows what the test
expected.

137
00:08:50,327 --> 00:08:53,611
The plus line shows what the function
returned.

138
00:08:54,220 --> 00:08:58,595
Now she changes line 13, to add U S D
before the amount.

139
00:08:59,187 --> 00:09:02,059
She runs the new test again, and it
passes.

140
00:09:02,079 --> 00:09:07,507
This is test first thinking: a test that
fails before the change and passes after

141
00:09:07,527 --> 00:09:09,727
it proves the change did its job.

142
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:11,334
One more run.

143
00:09:11,354 --> 00:09:17,700
Her test file from step 3 still expects
4.00, so it now fails on the new module.

144
00:09:17,733 --> 00:09:22,863
The rule changed on purpose, so this old
test is out of date.

145
00:09:22,883 --> 00:09:25,987
Asha updates it to expect U S D 4.00.

146
00:09:26,007 --> 00:09:29,870
When a rule changes, its tests change with
it.

147
00:09:30,487 --> 00:09:31,176
Quick check.

148
00:09:31,196 --> 00:09:34,720
You write a test for a new rule, before
you change the code.

149
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:37,379
What should happen the first time you run
it?

150
00:09:48,747 --> 00:09:48,876
B.

151
00:09:48,896 --> 00:09:49,549
It fails.

152
00:09:49,569 --> 00:09:52,691
The code does not follow the new rule yet.

153
00:09:52,711 --> 00:09:54,786
After the change, it passes.

154
00:09:55,387 --> 00:09:55,964
Breather.

155
00:09:55,984 --> 00:09:59,413
Now Asha puts everything together, in one
test file.

156
00:10:04,150 --> 00:10:05,672
Back to the mission.

157
00:10:05,692 --> 00:10:07,753
Lines 5 and 9 test the tax.

158
00:10:07,773 --> 00:10:10,760
Line 11 rounds the decimal total first.

159
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:15,845
Line 15 is the updated money test, with
the new U S D label.

160
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:21,332
Lines 18 to 26 test the boxes: normal
values, and then the edges.

161
00:10:21,352 --> 00:10:24,033
A test can hold several asserts.

162
00:10:35,413 --> 00:10:39,031
Five dots, one for each test function, and
five passed.

163
00:10:39,051 --> 00:10:42,403
A test with three asserts still counts as
one test.

164
00:10:42,433 --> 00:10:46,100
Now, after every change, Asha runs one
command.

165
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:47,433
Mission complete!

166
00:10:47,467 --> 00:10:53,145
Next, Asha asks an AI assistant to write
tests too, one stage at a time.

167
00:10:53,717 --> 00:10:58,706
This chapter's AI skill: check that the
AI's tests really test something.

168
00:10:58,726 --> 00:11:03,167
A test that can never fail looks exactly
like a test that passes.

169
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:05,985
So break a copy of the code on purpose.

170
00:11:06,005 --> 00:11:07,568
Good tests fail on it.

171
00:11:07,588 --> 00:11:09,654
And never paste private data.

172
00:11:10,253 --> 00:11:11,415
Stage 1: plan.

173
00:11:11,435 --> 00:11:17,321
Asha asks for the inputs to test, each
with its exact expected result.

174
00:11:17,341 --> 00:11:18,333
No code yet.

175
00:11:18,367 --> 00:11:24,157
The AI lists a part box, an exact
division, zero, and the edge of one box.

176
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:27,973
Asha works out every expected result
herself, by hand.

177
00:11:28,587 --> 00:11:29,736
Stage 2: write.

178
00:11:29,756 --> 00:11:34,413
Asha writes two tests herself: 24
biscuits, and 40 biscuits.

179
00:11:34,433 --> 00:11:37,687
She asks the AI what they do not test yet.

180
00:11:37,733 --> 00:11:41,604
The AI names zero, and the edge between 12
and 13.

181
00:11:41,624 --> 00:11:44,949
She will check the AI's file for those
too.

182
00:11:45,553 --> 00:11:46,962
Stage 3: generate.

183
00:11:46,982 --> 00:11:52,833
Asha asks the AI for a test file that
catches any mistake in boxes needed.

184
00:11:52,867 --> 00:11:56,512
The AI writes three tests, and makes a big
promise.

185
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:00,543
On Asha's working module, all three pass.

186
00:12:00,563 --> 00:12:02,727
Look at the two box tests.

187
00:12:02,747 --> 00:12:04,826
Line 10 uses 24 biscuits.

188
00:12:04,846 --> 00:12:09,277
And the test on line 14 calls boxes needed
with zero.

189
00:12:20,647 --> 00:12:20,878
No.

190
00:12:20,898 --> 00:12:24,399
24 divides exactly, so even round gives 2.

191
00:12:24,419 --> 00:12:29,011
And line 15 has no assert, so that test
can never fail.

192
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:30,727
Stage 4: test.

193
00:12:30,747 --> 00:12:35,960
Asha asks which of the AI's tests should
fail on her broken copy.

194
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,170
This time the AI admits it: none of them.

195
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:44,596
So she asks for a better file: exact
values, normal cases and edge cases, one

196
00:12:44,616 --> 00:12:45,932
behaviour per test.

197
00:12:46,553 --> 00:12:52,037
She makes a broken copy of her finished
module, with round on line 18, and runs

198
00:12:52,057 --> 00:12:52,873
them anyway.

199
00:12:52,893 --> 00:12:53,778
Three passed.

200
00:12:53,798 --> 00:12:56,425
The tests did not see the broken code.

201
00:12:57,020 --> 00:13:00,395
Then she runs her own two tests on the
same copy.

202
00:13:00,415 --> 00:13:04,136
The test for 40 biscuits fails, as a good
test should.

203
00:13:04,753 --> 00:13:05,913
Stage 5: debug.

204
00:13:05,933 --> 00:13:09,690
The AI writes the better file, with seven
tests.

205
00:13:09,710 --> 00:13:15,433
But on Asha's working module, one test
fails: test two items, on line 11.

206
00:13:16,053 --> 00:13:19,703
She pastes the failing test and the values
from the report.

207
00:13:19,733 --> 00:13:23,183
She asks whether the code or the test is
wrong.

208
00:13:23,203 --> 00:13:27,613
The AI reads the values: the test compares
decimals exactly.

209
00:13:27,633 --> 00:13:29,237
Round the total first.

210
00:13:29,853 --> 00:13:35,208
With the total rounded on line 11, all
seven tests pass on the working module.

211
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:40,862
And on the broken copy, two tests fail: 40
biscuits and 13 biscuits.

212
00:13:40,882 --> 00:13:43,690
Now the tests can see the broken code.

213
00:13:44,287 --> 00:13:45,833
Stage 6: improve.

214
00:13:45,853 --> 00:13:48,597
Asha asks for one improvement.

215
00:13:48,633 --> 00:13:54,923
The AI says: delete the tests for 24 and
for zero, because 40 and 13 already check

216
00:13:54,943 --> 00:13:55,846
rounding up.

217
00:13:56,453 --> 00:14:01,642
Asha remembers her own bug from Chapter
18: double slash, then plus 1.

218
00:14:01,662 --> 00:14:03,800
It was wrong for 24 biscuits.

219
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:07,148
She makes a second broken copy with that
bug.

220
00:14:07,753 --> 00:14:10,456
Asha tries the AI's shorter file on it.

221
00:14:10,476 --> 00:14:11,294
Five passed.

222
00:14:11,314 --> 00:14:15,204
Without those two tests, her old bug would
slip through.

223
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:21,656
The full file catches it: 24 biscuits
gives 3, and zero biscuits gives 1.

224
00:14:21,676 --> 00:14:23,661
So Asha keeps every test.

225
00:14:23,681 --> 00:14:25,506
The AI was wrong again.

226
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:26,953
Quick check.

227
00:14:26,973 --> 00:14:28,802
All of an AI's tests pass.

228
00:14:28,822 --> 00:14:30,793
What is the best next check?

229
00:14:42,147 --> 00:14:42,263
A.

230
00:14:42,283 --> 00:14:44,996
Run them on a copy you broke on purpose.

231
00:14:45,016 --> 00:14:47,115
Good tests fail on broken code.

232
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:48,303
Breather.

233
00:14:48,323 --> 00:14:53,928
You watched Asha check every AI test, and
catch two weak suggestions by breaking the

234
00:14:53,948 --> 00:14:55,000
code on purpose.

235
00:14:55,033 --> 00:14:58,358
Next, the mistakes beginners make with
tests.

236
00:15:03,083 --> 00:15:03,857
Mistake 1.

237
00:15:03,877 --> 00:15:06,396
Asha runs this file with pytest.

238
00:15:06,416 --> 00:15:09,332
Remember the naming rule from step 3.

239
00:15:20,713 --> 00:15:23,523
The function name, tea tax, on line 5.

240
00:15:23,543 --> 00:15:29,183
It does not start with test and an
underscore, so pytest skips it, even when

241
00:15:29,203 --> 00:15:31,417
you give it this file by name.

242
00:15:31,437 --> 00:15:33,130
Rename it test tea tax.

243
00:15:33,753 --> 00:15:37,363
You have seen mistakes 2 and 3 in the AI's
files.

244
00:15:37,383 --> 00:15:40,029
A test with no assert always passes.

245
00:15:40,049 --> 00:15:46,103
And an exact comparison of decimals can
fail on correct code: round the result, or

246
00:15:46,123 --> 00:15:46,917
use approx.

247
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:48,309
Mistake 4.

248
00:15:48,329 --> 00:15:52,599
Look at the import on line 2, and the test
on line 6.

249
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:09,032
Boxes needed is never imported, so the
test stops with a Name Error.

250
00:16:09,052 --> 00:16:10,300
Add it to line 2.

251
00:16:10,333 --> 00:16:14,632
The report points at line 6 of the test
file, not at the module.

252
00:16:14,652 --> 00:16:18,546
A Name Error inside a test means the test
itself is wrong.

253
00:16:19,117 --> 00:16:19,592
First.

254
00:16:19,612 --> 00:16:23,061
Money of minus 2 returns U S D minus 2.00.

255
00:16:23,081 --> 00:16:25,125
How should a refund look?

256
00:16:25,145 --> 00:16:28,594
Write a test for the label you would want.

257
00:16:39,947 --> 00:16:44,215
Decide the rule first, then write the
test, then change the code.

258
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:45,244
Second.

259
00:16:45,264 --> 00:16:46,450
Ten tests all pass.

260
00:16:46,470 --> 00:16:50,893
How could you find out whether they would
catch a mistake in with tax?

261
00:17:02,247 --> 00:17:05,128
Try it with a broken copy after the video.

262
00:17:05,717 --> 00:17:06,545
Let's recap.

263
00:17:06,565 --> 00:17:10,221
A test compares a result with the answer
you expect.

264
00:17:10,241 --> 00:17:15,806
Assert stops the program when it fails,
and pytest names each failure, its line

265
00:17:15,826 --> 00:17:16,867
and its values.

266
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:22,370
Test normal values and edge cases, and
compare decimals with round or approx.

267
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:23,802
Write the test first.

268
00:17:23,822 --> 00:17:28,070
And break a copy of the code on purpose:
good tests fail on it.

269
00:17:28,687 --> 00:17:30,016
Mission complete!

270
00:17:30,036 --> 00:17:33,669
You earned 100 XP and the Quality Guard
badge.

271
00:17:35,917 --> 00:17:39,795
Now it is your turn: eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

272
00:17:39,815 --> 00:17:44,937
Three warm-up tasks, three apply tasks,
and two stretch tasks.

273
00:17:44,967 --> 00:17:48,762
In the last one, you fix an AI's tests,
like Asha did.

274
00:17:48,782 --> 00:17:51,800
Try each task before you open the
solution.

275
00:17:52,383 --> 00:17:54,740
Asha's tests guard three small tools.

276
00:17:54,760 --> 00:17:58,143
But what about a whole program, with
several modules?

277
00:17:58,167 --> 00:18:02,783
In Chapter 20, the Boss Level, you will
build a quiz app for the cafe.

278
00:18:02,803 --> 00:18:08,214
You will write its tests first, let an AI
help with the code, and let the tests be

279
00:18:08,234 --> 00:18:08,877
the judge.

280
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:10,559
Do the warm-up tasks now.

281
00:18:10,579 --> 00:18:12,440
They take about ten minutes.

282
00:18:12,467 --> 00:18:14,158
See you in Chapter 20.
